# The five minute trading loop. One wake up is one tick for all five books.
# Read by scripts/gen_launchd.py, which writes the plist. Do not hand edit the
# plist, edit this and generate again.

[job]
name = tick
exit_timeout = 240
run_at_load = false

[program]
/bin/bash
{ROOT}/agent/run_tick.sh

[environment]
TZ = America/New_York
PATH = /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin
AGENTIC_TRADING_ROOT = {ROOT}

[schedule]
# The two morning filing sweeps.
at 07:00 on weekdays
at 07:30 on weekdays
# The pre-open half hour, every single minute. This is the pacing rule and not a
# preference: agent/preopen.py may send four historical requests a minute and a
# tick is over in a second or two, so a tick can never send more than four.
# Twenty seven wake ups between 09:00 and 09:26 pay for about a hundred
# requests, which is what the morning needs. Five minute wake ups pay for about
# twenty, and most of the candidate list would reach 09:35 with nothing behind
# it. See docs/PREOPEN_FLOW.md.
every 1 minute from 09:00 to 09:26 on weekdays
# The trading day itself. 09:25 is five minutes before the open, so the first
# tick has already checked Gateway and the MCP server before anything matters.
# It ends at 16:05 rather than 16:00 because the tick at or after the close is
# the one that writes each book's end of day line, and a job that fires exactly
# on the close can land a second early.
every 5 minutes from 09:25 to 16:05 on weekdays
# Book C sweeps SEC Form 4 filings again, for tomorrow morning.
at 16:30 on weekdays

[comment]
The five minute trading loop, run by launchd. One wake up is one tick for all
five books in config/books.yaml.

It runs {ROOT}/agent/run_tick.sh every five minutes from 09:25 to 16:05, Monday
to Friday, every minute from 09:00 to 09:26 for the pre-open run, plus three
extra times a day for the filing sweeps. That is 110 wake ups a day and 550
entries below. 09:25 falls in both morning grids and is written once, which is
why the two grids add 26 rather than 27.

The pre-open minutes are the reason this job is not simply a five minute grid.
agent/preopen.py pulls each new candidate's history at no more than four
requests a minute, and IB Gateway allows about sixty historical requests in any
ten minutes. Spending that budget between 09:00 and 09:26 means it is not being
spent in the five minutes around the open, where it is scarcest and where the
pick is made. A tick lasts a second or two, so waking every minute cannot
overrun the pacing rule; waking every five minutes buys about a fifth of what
the morning needs.

The three extra times, and which book each is for:

  07:00  book C sweeps SEC Form 4 filings, agent/sweep_insider.py
  07:30  book D sweeps Congress disclosures, agent/sweep_congress.py
  16:30  book C sweeps again for tomorrow morning

What it runs is dry run only. Every book in config/books.yaml is in dry_run
mode, so a loaded job works out what each of them would trade and sends
nothing. run_tick.sh clears AGENTIC_TRADING_LIVE_ORDERS before every tick.

Worth knowing: if the Mac is asleep at one of these times, launchd runs the job
once when it wakes up, and there is no setting here that turns that off. It is
harmless, because agent/loop.py reads the real clock and works out which part of
the day it is actually in. A tick meant for 10:15 that lands at 14:00 does the
14:00 thing. That is exactly why the phase comes from the clock rather than from
which slot woke it.
